WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.838
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level B two.

2
00:00:06.138 --> 00:00:09.640
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:15.692
Unit twenty-nine: Ellipsis and Substitution. Today you will avoid repetition naturally.

4
00:00:15.992 --> 00:00:18.564
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.791
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:24.500
So.

7
00:00:25.517 --> 00:00:27.493
So means replaces a clause.

8
00:00:28.083 --> 00:00:29.189
I hope so.

9
00:00:29.783 --> 00:00:31.948
Your turn. Say: So.

10
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:35.639
So.

11
00:00:36.383 --> 00:00:37.384
Do so.

12
00:00:38.383 --> 00:00:41.730
Do so means replaces a verb phrase in formal English.

13
00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:44.467
He asked me to wait and I did so.

14
00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:47.608
Your turn. Say: Do so.

15
00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:51.305
Do so.

16
00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:52.807
One.

17
00:00:53.817 --> 00:00:56.060
One means replaces a singular noun.

18
00:00:56.650 --> 00:00:58.104
I prefer the red one.

19
00:00:58.717 --> 00:01:00.556
Your turn. Say: One.

20
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.053
One.

21
00:01:04.817 --> 00:01:05.678
Ones.

22
00:01:06.683 --> 00:01:08.962
Ones means replaces plural nouns.

23
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:11.085
I like the blue ones.

24
00:01:11.683 --> 00:01:13.813
Your turn. Say: Ones.

25
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:17.586
Ones.

26
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:19.119
Not.

27
00:01:20.117 --> 00:01:22.569
Not means replaces a negative clause.

28
00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:24.335
I hope not.

29
00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:27.742
Your turn. Say: Not.

30
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:31.543
Not.

31
00:01:32.283 --> 00:01:33.110
Omit.

32
00:01:34.117 --> 00:01:35.629
Omit means to leave out.

33
00:01:36.217 --> 00:01:37.891
Omit the repeated words.

34
00:01:38.483 --> 00:01:40.404
Your turn. Say: Omit.

35
00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:44.245
Omit.

36
00:01:44.983 --> 00:01:46.600
Avoid repetition.

37
00:01:47.583 --> 00:01:51.173
Avoid repetition means do not say the same words again.

38
00:01:51.783 --> 00:01:53.272
Avoid repetition.

39
00:01:53.883 --> 00:01:56.849
Your turn. Say: Avoid repetition.

40
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.212
Avoid repetition.

41
00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.102
Cohesion.

42
00:02:04.117 --> 00:02:06.778
Cohesion means how a text holds together.

43
00:02:07.383 --> 00:02:08.408
Cohesion.

44
00:02:09.017 --> 00:02:11.483
Your turn. Say: Cohesion.

45
00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:15.381
Cohesion.

46
00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:19.690
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:25.580
Visal, rewrite to avoid repetition: I like green tea and she likes green tea too.

48
00:02:26.683 --> 00:02:38.426
I like green tea, and so does she. Ellipsis leaves out words that are obvious, and substitution replaces them with so, one, do so or not.

49
00:02:39.517 --> 00:02:41.063
What are some examples?

50
00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:55.436
Are you coming? I hope so. I think not. I like the red car, but she prefers the blue one. I can swim, and so can my brother. He asked me to wait, and I did so.

51
00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:57.957
Are there limits?

52
00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:11.088
We omit only what the listener can recover. One replaces a countable noun, not an uncountable one. Too much ellipsis may confuse the reader. Good writers balance clarity and economy.

53
00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:13.722
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:14.583 --> 00:03:19.218
Grammar focus: Ellipsis and substitution: so, one, do so, not.

55
00:03:19.518 --> 00:03:20.694
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:21.383 --> 00:03:23.882
I like green tea, and so does she.

57
00:03:24.783 --> 00:03:27.700
I like the red car, but she prefers the blue one.

58
00:03:28.617 --> 00:03:30.630
Are you coming? I hope so.

59
00:03:31.567 --> 00:03:37.725
Use so, not, one / ones and do so to avoid repetition. Leave out words only when the meaning is clear.

60
00:03:38.483 --> 00:03:42.520
Be careful! Don't say: I hope it. / I think it.

61
00:03:44.467 --> 00:03:47.119
Say: I hope so. / I think so.

62
00:03:47.419 --> 00:03:48.525
Use so.

63
00:03:49.333 --> 00:03:52.183
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:52.967 --> 00:03:54.049
Saying Less.

65
00:03:54.349 --> 00:03:57.016
'Is it going to rain?' 'I hope not.'

66
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.195
I like the red car, but she prefers the blue one.

67
00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.455
I can swim, and so can my brother.

68
00:04:05.467 --> 00:04:07.606
He asked me to wait, and I did so.

69
00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.993
Ellipsis helps cohesion, but too much can confuse the reader.

70
00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:16.649
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:17.483 --> 00:04:19.999
'Is she coming?' 'I think blank.'

72
00:04:20.299 --> 00:04:22.849
A, so. B, that. C, it.

73
00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.993
The answer is A: so.

74
00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:31.428
I like green tea and blank does she.

75
00:04:31.728 --> 00:04:34.813
A, too. B, also. C, so.

76
00:04:38.367 --> 00:04:39.937
The answer is C: so.

77
00:04:41.083 --> 00:04:42.665
I prefer the blue blank.

78
00:04:42.965 --> 00:04:45.539
A, one. B, it. C, them.

79
00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:50.740
The answer is A: one.

80
00:04:51.883 --> 00:04:53.651
Which avoids repetition?

81
00:04:53.951 --> 00:05:00.390
A, I can swim, and he can swim.. B, I swim, he swim.. C, I can swim, and so can he..

82
00:05:03.933 --> 00:05:07.338
The answer is C: I can swim, and so can he..

83
00:05:08.483 --> 00:05:11.388
According to the reading, what does the brother do?

84
00:05:11.688 --> 00:05:15.214
A, swims. B, sings. C, runs.

85
00:05:18.767 --> 00:05:21.045
The answer is A: swims.

86
00:05:22.233 --> 00:05:25.629
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:26.483 --> 00:05:32.236
Rewrite to avoid repetition: 'I like green tea and she likes green tea too.'

88
00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:42.666
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:42.966 --> 00:05:52.829
I like green tea, and so does she. I replaced the second clause with so does she, because the meaning is clear and the sentence is shorter.

90
00:05:53.129 --> 00:05:54.873
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:05:55.783 --> 00:06:02.035
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and ellipsis and substitution: so, one, do so, not.

92
00:06:02.335 --> 00:06:07.414
Today's value is precision of language. Choose your words carefully so others understand you.

93
00:06:07.714 --> 00:06:10.331
See you in Unit thirty. Goodbye!

94
00:06:10.631 --> 00:06:13.434
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

